# Python Environment Standard

> **Auto-distributed via simpl_knowledge. Propose edits with a PR on `simpl-techs/simpl_knowledge`.**

This standard applies to every Python repo at simpl (services, libraries, scripts).

## The rule

- **Create** the Python environment with **Conda** (from a checked-in `environment.yml`).
- **Manage** dependencies inside that environment with **Poetry** (`pyproject.toml` + `poetry.lock`).
- **Run** anything (tests, lint, scripts, the app) via the **local Poetry virtualenv** that `poetry install` creates inside the activated Conda env. Never use a globally installed Python or a one-off `pip install`.

Conda owns the interpreter and system-level toolchain (e.g. Poetry itself, sometimes native deps). Poetry owns the Python package graph. Don't mix the two.

## Required files in every Python repo

- `environment.yml` — Conda env spec. Pins Python version and installs Poetry. The Conda env name should match the repo name (e.g. `simpl_api`).
- `pyproject.toml` — Poetry project + dependencies.
- `poetry.lock` — committed. The lockfile is the source of truth for reproducible installs.
- `README.md` — must include the setup section shown below so a new contributor (human or agent) can bootstrap correctly.

## Canonical README setup section

Every Python repo's `README.md` must include a section equivalent to this (adjust the env name and project name):

````markdown
## Installation

### Prerequisites

- [Conda](https://docs.conda.io/en/latest/miniconda.html) or [Anaconda](https://www.anaconda.com/)
- [Poetry](https://python-poetry.org/) (will be installed via Conda)

### Setup

1. **Create Conda environment:**
   ```bash
   conda env create -f environment.yml
   conda activate <repo_name>
   ```

2. **Install Poetry dependencies:**
   ```bash
   poetry install
   ```

3. **Set up environment variables:**
   ```bash
   cp .env.example .env
   # Edit .env with your values
   ```

## Development Workflow

### Activate Environment

Always activate the Conda environment before working:
```bash
conda activate <repo_name>
```

### Running Tests

```bash
poetry run pytest
```

With coverage report:
```bash
poetry run pytest --cov=src --cov-report=html
```

## Linting and Formatting

Check code quality:
```bash
poetry run ruff check .
```

Auto-fix issues:
```bash
poetry run ruff check --fix .
```

Format code:
```bash
poetry run ruff format .
```
````

If a repo deviates from this layout, flag it to the human before adding more drift.

## Rules for agents

These are **non-negotiable** when an agent works in a Python repo at simpl.

### 1. Always use the local Poetry virtualenv

The project's Python environment is the **Conda env whose name matches the repo** (e.g. `simpl_outreach` repo → `simpl_outreach` env, `simpl_tracker` → `simpl_tracker`). Activate it with `conda activate <repo_name>` before any Python work. Never run Python tools against the **base** Conda env, the system Python, or any other repo's env — wrong interpreter and wrong package set are the single most common source of breakage.

When running tests, linters, type-checkers, the app, or any Python tool:

- ✅ `poetry run pytest`
- ✅ `poetry run ruff check .`
- ✅ `poetry run python -m <module>`
- ❌ `pytest` (uses whatever Python is on PATH — wrong env, missing deps)
- ❌ `python -m pytest` (same problem)
- ❌ `pip install <anything>` (bypasses Poetry; corrupts the lockfile relationship)

If `poetry run ...` fails because the env isn't installed yet, run `poetry install` first (inside the activated Conda env). Never fall back to a global Python.

### 2. Never edit `pyproject.toml` or `poetry.lock` by hand to change dependencies

To add, remove, or update packages, **always** go through Poetry's CLI so the lockfile stays consistent:

| Goal                          | Command                                |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Add a runtime dependency      | `poetry add <pkg>`                     |
| Add a dev-only dependency     | `poetry add --group dev <pkg>`         |
| Add with version constraint   | `poetry add "<pkg>>=1.2,<2"`           |
| Remove a dependency           | `poetry remove <pkg>`                  |
| Update a single dependency    | `poetry update <pkg>`                  |
| Update everything in lockfile | `poetry update`                        |
| Install from lockfile         | `poetry install`                       |
| Re-lock after manual edits    | `poetry lock --no-update`              |

The only times it's acceptable to hand-edit `pyproject.toml` are for non-dependency metadata (project name, scripts, tool config like `[tool.ruff]`, `[tool.pytest.ini_options]`). Even then, never touch `[tool.poetry.dependencies]` or `[tool.poetry.group.*.dependencies]` manually.

`poetry.lock` is **never** hand-edited — let Poetry regenerate it.

### 3. Confirm with the user before installing anything into the project env

Any change to the project's Python packages requires explicit human approval first — runtime deps, dev/test deps, type stubs, anything. Specifically the agent MUST stop and ask before running:

- `poetry add ...` / `poetry add --group dev ...`
- `poetry update` / `poetry update <pkg>`
- `poetry remove ...`
- `pip install ...` (which is already forbidden — see section 1)

The agent does not silently mutate `pyproject.toml`, `poetry.lock`, or the installed env. "It's just a small dev tool" is not a reason to skip the ask — installs that look harmless still touch the lockfile and force a re-resolve.

#### Carve-out: throwaway sandbox env

If the agent needs to try a package, reproduce a behavior, or smoke-test something **without** modifying the project's env, it MAY create a **separate, temporary** Python env outside the project (e.g. `python -m venv /tmp/simpl-sandbox-<short-name>` or a fresh Conda env with a clearly temporary name), install whatever it needs there, do its work, and **delete the env when done**.

Rules for the carve-out:

- The temp env is **never** the project's env. It is created outside the repo and never named after the repo.
- The agent **announces it explicitly** to the user before creating it ("I'm spinning up a throwaway sandbox at `<path>` to test X; I'll delete it after").
- Nothing the agent does in the sandbox touches `pyproject.toml`, `poetry.lock`, the project's Conda env, or `~/.conda/envs/<repo_name>`.
- The agent deletes the sandbox before reporting the task as done.

This is the **only** way the agent installs a Python package without first getting human approval.

### 4. Don't change the Python version casually

The Python version is pinned in `environment.yml` and reflected in `pyproject.toml`'s `[tool.poetry.dependencies] python = "..."`. Bumping it is a coordinated change — propose it to the human, don't just edit the file.

## Quick troubleshooting

- **`poetry: command not found`** → Conda env isn't activated. `conda activate <repo_name>`.
- **`ModuleNotFoundError` for a dep that's in `pyproject.toml`** → run `poetry install` (lockfile and env out of sync).
- **`poetry install` reports lock is out of date** → someone hand-edited `pyproject.toml`. Run `poetry lock --no-update` to regenerate, then `poetry install`. Flag the manual edit to the human.
- **Tests pass locally but fail in CI** → first check whether CI activates the Conda env and runs via `poetry run`. Most simpl-side breakage is "wrong interpreter".

## When in doubt

Ask the human before deviating. A misconfigured environment is the single most common source of "works on my machine" bugs we hit.